            0.659,
            0.19
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "\\overline{x}=\\frac{\\Sigma f_{i}x_{i}}{\\Sigma f_{i}}=\\frac{1779}{30}=59.3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.203,
            0.525,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore, the mean marks obtained is 59.3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.231,
            0.902,
            0.296
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In most of our real life situations, data is usually so large that to make a meaningful study it needs to be condensed as grouped data. So, we need to convert given ungrouped data into grouped data and devise some method to find its mean."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.303,
            0.903,
            0.434
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us convert the ungrouped data of Example 1 into grouped data by forming class-intervals of width, say 15. Remember that, while allocating frequencies to each class-interval, students falling in any upper class-limit would be considered in the next class, e.g., 4 students who have obtained 40 marks would be considered in the classinterval 40-55 and not in 25-40. With this convention in our mind, let us form a grouped frequency distribution table (see Table 13.2)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.473,
            0.897,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_caption",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.441,
                0.445,
                0.559,
                0.464
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 13.2"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.101,
                0.473,
                0.897,
                0.553
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_table_8.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>Class interval</td><td>10 - 25</td><td>25 - 40</td><td>40 - 55</td><td>55 - 70</td><td>70 - 85</td><td>85 - 100</td></tr><tr><td>Number of students</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>7</td><td>6</td><td>6</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.571,
            0.903,
            0.716
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, for each class-interval, we require a point which would serve as the representative of the whole class. It "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "i \\hat { s }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " assumed that the frequency of each classinterval is centred around its mid-point. So the mid-point (or class mark) of each class can be chosen to represent the observations falling in the class. Recall that we find the mid-point of a class (or its class mark) by finding the average of its upper and lower limits. That is,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.238,
            0.723,
            0.761,
            0.765
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "{ \\mathrm { C l a s s ~ m a r k } } = { \\frac { { \\mathrm { U p p e r ~ c l a s s ~ l i m i t } } + { \\mathrm { L o w e r ~ c l a s s ~ l i m i t } } } { 2 } }"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.776,
            0.902,
            0.903
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "With reference to Table 13.2, for the class 10-25, the class mark is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 0 + 2 5 } { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , i.e., 17.5. Similarly, we can find the class marks of the remaining class intervals. We put them in Table 13.3. These class marks serve as our "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x _ { i } ^ { \\mathrm { ~ \\scriptsize ~ \\dot { ~ } } } \\mathrm { S }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Now, in general, for the ith class interval, we have the frequency "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f _ { i }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " corresponding to the class mark "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x _ { i ^ { * } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " We can now proceed to compute the mean in the same manner as in Example 1."
            }
          ]
        },
